#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176753} :activity/id #uuid "a82f82dc-e433-5288-bb71-1269a1685809" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048516 :topic/id #uuid "a5effb25-537a-5891-b350-6a8d3f357154" :topic/math-academy-id 3642} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175958 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175804 :question/id #uuid "5e71fcd7-b782-5399-979f-fd62607b5653" :question/math-academy-id "e-7421"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33fca834-0063-5252-832f-5efb4be6e599" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426786}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions Using the Properties of Expected Value"} :step/id #uuid "7c87517d-0124-503b-ad8d-9d75e2bcf4c6" :step/math-academy-id 27507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175991 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175842 :question/id #uuid "46566681-c2e4-53e1-83c4-d438cabc8066" :question/math-academy-id "e-9106"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c105dd8-6739-5889-a529-9182ea814d76" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048317}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416522}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "e4a2022f-0bfe-53a7-a3a3-924b5ed19a7c" :step/math-academy-id 33947 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176751}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176751 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177003 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over $S.$\n\nThe values $\\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm E[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment**, respectively. \n\nIt's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm E[X^3]$ is the **third moment. **\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X] =  \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x)$ is the first moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^2] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^2 \\cdot f(x)$ is the second moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^3] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^3 \\cdot f(x)$ is the third moment\n\n- $\\,\\cdots$\n\nIn general, the $n$th  moment is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm E[X^n] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^n \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nThe concept of a moment will become important later. In particular, we'll soon see how $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ can be used to compute the **variance** of a random variable.\n\nFinally, note that $\\textrm E[X], \\textrm E[X^2], \\textrm E[X^3],$ etc. are sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moment. \n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." :tutorial/id #uuid "0f377288-40af-50ed-b77c-90edf07bb005" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5499 :tutorial/title "The Moments of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "77279341-1e22-5bb1-bd2c-8981a3235685" :step/math-academy-id 34228 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177004 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a discrete random variable $X$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of $X,$ denoted $\\textrm E[X],$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is the probability mass function of $X.$ \n\nTherefore, we can calculate the expected value of the random variable $X^2$ as\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X^2] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x^2 \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that the random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nIn this case, $\\textrm E[X^2]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.5+9\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** When computing $\\textrm E[X^2],$ it's important to remember that we still evaluate the probability mass function as $f(x),$ *not* $f(x^2).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "544551ff-1bd9-5834-8bed-084fe6b81425" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5503 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e5dae560-1167-5534-8639-30d7fa5c56b5" :step/math-academy-id 34238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176741}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176042 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175689 :question/id #uuid "69fdace0-5c5b-519b-b03b-40fd523915f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10128"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebfe6309-b8a3-5e4c-b2ae-495a91bcc433" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426787}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Higher Moments of a Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "f44e66b4-ae69-5a8a-b61a-38a70551aa2f" :step/math-academy-id 38212 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176685}}] :activity/title "Moments of Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176159}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426789} :activity/id #uuid "0d55a9b0-574a-5d03-8ebf-81a57bd60984" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048516 :topic/id #uuid "a5effb25-537a-5891-b350-6a8d3f357154" :topic/math-academy-id 3642} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426788 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b84e972-91af-5283-be7d-7b174d7aa34a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "542" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426760} {:answer/id #uuid "161b6b27-31a6-5f60-9425-0f9c32ebfa5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "590" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426763} {:answer/id #uuid "b3809182-32d0-5d1f-aca6-70df8dd13f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "592" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426773} {:answer/id #uuid "db888a76-ff37-54a2-9ae5-b989f39555be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "594" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426778} {:answer/id #uuid "f53461b4-981e-55c3-801a-8234df8336a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "562" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426773} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a85f0d-532b-5b07-b202-73baf26d36cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "86bc7dc6-b40a-53a5-81fd-6b94c073d41e" :question/math-academy-id "q-110008" :question/problem "Compute $\\text{E}[(6Y+4)^{2}]$ if $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=12$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding out the square inside the expected value, using the properties of the expected value, and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(6Y+4)^{2}] & =\\text{E}[36{Y}^{2}+48Y+16] \\\\  & =36\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]+48\\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[16] \\\\  & =36\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]+48\\text{E}[Y]+16 \\\\  & =36(12)+48(3)+16 \\\\  & =592.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "18de0dd1-618a-5e31-817b-0e756daf3147" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e36a1d7-0791-58f7-8982-868304ac8df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426761} {:answer/id #uuid "46a8ef29-eaf0-516d-9633-2006fce4fb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426767} {:answer/id #uuid "6c249428-8d08-51bf-ae81-1bb5765af7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426769} {:answer/id #uuid "739fc267-7138-5a80-afd7-189d677e4a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426770} {:answer/id #uuid "c13fd77c-9ad1-5778-b83e-31cad41c6866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426769} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eed2828-4a7b-5794-87c9-0670f96099bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "07220d09-adbb-5d69-80d7-bbbef5274be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-83949" :question/problem "Compute $\\text{E}[(A+1)(A-3)]$ if $\\text{E}[{A}^{2}]=7$ and $\\text{E}[A]=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding out the square inside the expected value, using the properties of the expected value, and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[(A+1)(A-3)] & =\\text{E}[{A}^{2}-2A-3] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{A}^{2}]+\\text{E}[-2A]+\\text{E}[-3] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{A}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[A]-3 \\\\  & =7-2(2)-3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d79ed21b-cce2-5cca-a6b9-df978d6fbdd6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426790}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14f52b46-c4f1-578f-bb31-b313900911f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "226" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426762} {:answer/id #uuid "436176b4-0742-570b-8b2d-7e7ea6f2954d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "235" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426765} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcb654d-d8e4-57d2-ab07-b403eb97d3e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "230" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426768} {:answer/id #uuid "8a49c95d-3f9c-54fb-ad81-6165ab7af9af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426771} {:answer/id #uuid "be60d140-3585-552d-83b8-46780f21eddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "233" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426774} :answer-field/id #uuid "81f9328d-36f0-5c65-8f7c-7e261e12c70a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "765be8ee-8b33-57cc-babb-5991ad40d4a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109311" :question/problem "Compute $\\text{E}[X(5X+2)-3]$ if $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=50$ and $\\text{E}[X]=-7.$" :question/worked-solution "Expanding out the product inside the expected value, using the properties of the expected value, and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X(5X+2)-3] & =\\text{E}[5{X}^{2}+2X-3] \\\\  & =5\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]+\\text{E}[-3] \\\\  & =5\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-3 \\\\  & =5(50)+2(-7)-3 \\\\  & =233.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "db3c4ac3-b4ba-5487-98a4-80e057758e86" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426788}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b48da996-83e4-5e76-885d-d2f866efdb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174515} {:answer/id #uuid "beb1e493-b90f-56f2-8837-460b1c3b8a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174516} {:answer/id #uuid "f94c187e-9eb8-5796-9ed1-fbaddd666f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174517} {:answer/id #uuid "59462356-ec93-5845-9eb3-8534e160e05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174518} {:answer/id #uuid "6380f064-7b18-5622-943f-749d9634d4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174519}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186174515} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe77e694-ed18-5bfe-9180-33e93463e7c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2d805fb0-bc76-54a9-a263-7551f00436aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-143751" :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{10}, & x=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}, & x=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ and sample space $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{4}]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each squared value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{10}+{1}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+{2}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{37}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "edc7c2d4-1f4f-5fa0-a157-630df08222ef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426792}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31869a6a-c7b3-5fd8-aa3e-284f3d59ab95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426764} {:answer/id #uuid "4386d36c-9dbd-5582-8c22-4504cf7121c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426766} {:answer/id #uuid "92d52443-3bd3-516d-bbf6-84b158e700ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426772} {:answer/id #uuid "cce46a42-c639-5945-9a8a-12511515e16d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426776} {:answer/id #uuid "daa8a404-d0e4-57d9-8714-4c89d9ef164f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426766} :answer-field/id #uuid "802686da-19e2-5e90-856b-10082948e6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "97f24d3e-df80-5494-a01f-c5ae043371c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143794" :question/problem "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.05$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ is a discrete random variable with probability mass function $f(x)$ and sample space $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{3}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each squared value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{3}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{3}\\cdot 0.05+{1}^{3}\\cdot 0.4+{2}^{3}\\cdot 0.1+{3}^{3}\\cdot 0.2+{4}^{3}\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.05+1\\cdot 0.4+8\\cdot 0.1+27\\cdot 0.2+64\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0+0.4+0.8+5.4+16 \\\\  & =22.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "febf35a0-8420-578a-9890-44f3cfbcd235"}] :activity/title "Moments of Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426759}]}
